WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.466 --> 00:00:03.340
SETH VARGO: Hi there.

00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:05.380
And welcome to the third
video in our series

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:07.270
on SRE and DevOps.

00:00:07.270 --> 00:00:09.370
My name is Seth, and
I'm a Developer Advocate

00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:12.400
at Google focused on
infrastructure and operations.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:14.230
LIZ FONG-JONES: And
I'm Liz, an SRE focused

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:16.360
on teaching Google Cloud
customers how to build

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:18.344
and operate reliable services.

00:00:18.344 --> 00:00:19.760
SETH VARGO: In the
previous video,

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:24.630
we discussed the differences
between SLIs, SLOs, and SLAs.

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:27.740
SLIs are quantitative
measurements, like latency.

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:30.550
SLOs are the amount
of time that an SLI

00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:32.500
can be out of specification.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:36.310
And SLAs are business agreements
with explicit consequences

00:00:36.310 --> 00:00:38.650
for failing to deliver service.

00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:41.690
But what stops teams from
breaking their agreed upon SLOs

00:00:41.690 --> 00:00:44.580
and forcing SREs
to work overtime?

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:46.710
It seems like a classic
DevOps problem where

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:48.900
product teams want
to ship new features,

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:51.630
but SREs need to
maintain a reliability.

00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:53.700
Is there anything
in the SRE program

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:55.532
that can help with
this classic problem?

00:00:55.532 --> 00:00:57.740
LIZ FONG-JONES: That's what
we use error budgets for.

00:00:57.740 --> 00:00:59.010
SETH VARGO: Error budgets?

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:01.480
LIZ FONG-JONES: Well, before
we talk about error budgets,

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:04.260
let's talk about risk
and availability.

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:07.290
As I mentioned in the previous
episode, trying to go for 100%

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:11.700
availability just isn't a good
idea because it's expensive.

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:13.050
It's technically complex.

00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:15.660
And in a lot of cases, it
winds up being the case

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:18.300
that users don't even see
the benefits of it because

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:20.640
of end reliability somewhere
else in this system.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:21.390
SETH VARGO: I see.

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:22.473
That makes a lot of sense.

00:01:22.473 --> 00:01:26.040
Because if my cellular
network is only 99% reliable,

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:29.160
but my service is
99.9% reliable,

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:32.310
my users are never going to
experience that additional 0.9%

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:34.650
of reliability because
they're cellular network

00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:36.787
is likely to fail
before my service does.

00:01:36.787 --> 00:01:37.620
LIZ FONG-JONES: Yes.

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:38.860
That's exactly correct.

00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:42.460
So while we want to reduce
the risk of system failures,

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:44.220
we have to accept
some degree of risk

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:46.662
in order to deliver these
products and features.

00:01:46.662 --> 00:01:48.120
SETH VARGO: But
how do we determine

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:50.790
how much risk a service
is willing to tolerate?

00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:53.070
LIZ FONG-JONES: So that's
a product decision.

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:55.410
So we have to work with the
product's management team

00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:57.870
to figure out what
is our explicit goal

00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:00.122
for the availability
target of our service.

00:02:00.122 --> 00:02:01.830
And there are many
things to think about,

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:05.040
like how much is it going
to cost to add extra fault

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:07.470
tolerance, or to add
extra testing time,

00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:09.600
or to reduce our
frequency of pushes,

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:12.030
or to increase how long
it takes for us to decide

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:15.120
that a release is good compared
to the benefits to the user

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:16.590
of increased reliability.

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:17.340
SETH VARGO: I see.

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:20.820
So the acceptable risk of
a system dictates the SLO,

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:24.120
and the SLO mathematically
defines the error budget.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:26.670
If the service incurs
too much downtime,

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:29.820
we have to reduce the risk to
remain within the SLO, which

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:31.650
might mean halting deployments.

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:34.230
If service owners
want to deliver

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:36.210
a lot of risky
features, they have

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:39.000
to be willing to accept
a much looser SLO.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.095
Because if they were
to choose a strict SLO,

00:02:41.095 --> 00:02:43.220
they would quickly exceed
their error budget, which

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:44.585
could halt future deployments.

00:02:44.585 --> 00:02:45.585
LIZ FONG-JONES: Exactly.

00:02:45.585 --> 00:02:47.610
So the main benefit
of an error budget

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:49.710
is that it's a quantitative
measurement that's

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:52.440
shared between the product
and SRE teams, which

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:54.840
means that we can balance
innovation and stability

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:56.079
to an appropriate level.

00:02:56.079 --> 00:02:57.870
SETH VARGO: So as long
as the SLOs are met,

00:02:57.870 --> 00:02:59.310
releases can continue.

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:02.320
But how do we know if an SLO
breach is about to occur?

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:04.920
LIZ FONG-JONES: So when we
defined earlier the expectation

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:06.900
of how much uptime a
service is going to have

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:08.430
and how we're going
to measure it,

00:03:08.430 --> 00:03:10.470
well, we need to actually
concretely implement

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:12.690
that using a
neutral third party,

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:14.050
like a monitoring system.

00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:15.425
SETH VARGO: Well,
and the metrics

00:03:15.425 --> 00:03:17.660
on that monitoring system,
those are the SLIs, right?

00:03:17.660 --> 00:03:18.660
LIZ FONG-JONES: Exactly.

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:20.520
And the difference
between the actual uptime

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:23.550
and the calculated target
uptime from our SLO

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:25.560
is the budget of how
much unavailability

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:27.480
that we can tolerate
for the system

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:30.840
to be stable over the
entire window of the SLO.

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:32.430
So we call this
the error budget.

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:34.424
If your SLIs are
failing all the time,

00:03:34.424 --> 00:03:36.840
then you're going to be burning
through your error budget.

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:40.350
And then eventually, you need
to stop your feature releases

00:03:40.350 --> 00:03:43.679
in order to focus instead on
making reliability improvements

00:03:43.679 --> 00:03:45.720
and restructuring your
application so that it can

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:48.036
meet your SLOs in the future.

00:03:48.036 --> 00:03:50.160
SETH VARGO: So who enforces
those policies, though?

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:54.270
Because couldn't a product team
just go over and break the SLO

00:03:54.270 --> 00:03:56.950
and force the SREs
to work overtime?

00:03:56.950 --> 00:03:58.470
LIZ FONG-JONES:
So this is why we

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:01.230
need to have executive
buy-in for error budgets.

00:04:01.230 --> 00:04:02.880
If the SRE teams
don't have the ability

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:05.005
to enforce the error budgets,
then the whole system

00:04:05.005 --> 00:04:06.090
is going to break down.

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:10.140
So some teams just allow
for a limited number

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:13.230
of tokens or golden bullets
that you can hand out to a vice

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:14.740
president, for example.

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:16.200
So if a product
team really wants

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:17.670
to get that critical
feature out,

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:19.544
well, they're going to
have to ask their vice

00:04:19.544 --> 00:04:21.089
president for a
one-time exception,

00:04:21.089 --> 00:04:23.580
and they'll only get a
certain number per year.

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:24.330
SETH VARGO: I see.

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:27.390
But what about things that are
outside of the product team

00:04:27.390 --> 00:04:30.300
that aren't necessarily
buggy code for my developers,

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:32.940
like someone cuts
an undersea cable,

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:36.180
or there's a catastrophic
failure at a data center?

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:38.010
Those shouldn't impact
my error budget.

00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:39.522
It wasn't my fault.

00:04:39.522 --> 00:04:40.980
LIZ FONG-JONES: So
this is why it's

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:43.410
important to have error
budgets from top to bottom

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:45.150
for everything in your stack.

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:48.060
That way you can figure out how
much error budget you allocate

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:51.120
to your dependencies and how
much error budget is reserved

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:52.920
for your developers to spend.

00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:55.920
And this is another reason why
targeting 100% availability

00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:58.200
isn't realistic, because
all of your dependencies

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:00.450
are not 100% available either.

00:05:00.450 --> 00:05:02.170
SETH VARGO: That
makes a lot of sense.

00:05:02.170 --> 00:05:05.520
But what about other things
like restarting a failed service

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:08.160
or other kind of manual tasks?

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:10.255
Are those considered
part of the error budget?

00:05:10.255 --> 00:05:11.130
LIZ FONG-JONES: Yeah.

00:05:11.130 --> 00:05:14.430
So Seth, when you have
to do manual action

00:05:14.430 --> 00:05:17.150
to keep your system from
failing, in the wait

00:05:17.150 --> 00:05:18.900
before you actually
do that manual action,

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:21.120
you'll start burning
through your error budget.

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:23.370
But the actual act of
doing that manual work,

00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:24.570
we track that separately.

00:05:24.570 --> 00:05:26.250
And that's a concept
that we call toil.

00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:29.760
So we'll talk about that more
in detail in the next video.

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:30.690
SETH VARGO: Great.

00:05:30.690 --> 00:05:33.300
So risk and error budgets
are directly related

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:35.190
to many of the DevOps
principles that we've

00:05:35.190 --> 00:05:37.200
discussed in earlier episodes.

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:38.970
It clearly defines
that accidents

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:41.310
are normal by
quantifying accidents

00:05:41.310 --> 00:05:43.260
and risk through error budgets.

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:45.940
It also enforces that
change should be gradual

00:05:45.940 --> 00:05:48.460
because a non-gradual
change could quickly

00:05:48.460 --> 00:05:51.010
burn through the error budget
for a particular product

00:05:51.010 --> 00:05:53.500
breaking the SLO and
preventing further deployment

00:05:53.500 --> 00:05:55.670
for the quarter or for the year.

00:05:55.670 --> 00:05:56.930
This has really helped a lot.

00:05:56.930 --> 00:06:00.325
I think it's really clear why
we say that class SRE implements

00:06:00.325 --> 00:06:01.332
DevOps.

00:06:01.332 --> 00:06:03.290
LIZ FONG-JONES: Thanks,
everyone, for watching.

00:06:03.290 --> 00:06:06.324
Check the description below
for links and more information.

00:06:06.324 --> 00:06:07.990
Don't forget to
subscribe to the channel

00:06:07.990 --> 00:06:09.490
and stay tuned
for our next video

00:06:09.490 --> 00:06:12.060
where we talk
about toil budgets.

